<%# Desktop-only declaration for the admin wallet flows.

    WHY IT PAINTS INSTEAD OF WAITING FOR A CLICK. Every one of these pages
    ends in an irreversible on-chain action, and each makes the operator do
    real work first: vault_state asks for a pause reason that gets logged
    on-chain and then a confirm dialog naming the network; vault_init asks
    for four pubkeys and a threshold; the treasury page asks which pending
    transaction to co-sign. Learning "your device cannot do this" AFTER all
    of that is the moment somebody starts hunting for a workaround. So the
    page says it up front, and disables the buttons, and the click-time
    throw stays as the backstop for anything that changes in between.

    THE SENTENCE IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. It comes from
    window.walletProvider.desktopOnlyMessage(), the same source the throw
    uses, so the painted reason and the thrown reason can never drift.
    The layout inlines that method into its stub, which is what answers at
    parse time, and wallet_stub_parity_test.rb keeps the two agreeing.

    HIDDEN BY DEFAULT, revealed only on a phone. A desktop operator sees
    nothing new on the page. %>
<div id="wallet-desktop-only-notice"
     class="rounded-lg border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/80 dark:border-amber-700/40 dark:bg-amber-950/30 px-4 py-3 mb-6"
     hidden>
  <p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400/90 mb-1">
    Desktop only
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm text-secondary" data-desktop-only-message></p>
</div>

<script>
  (function () {
    function paintDesktopOnlyNotice() {
      var wp = window.walletProvider;
      if (!wp || typeof wp.isMobile !== 'function' || !wp.isMobile()) return;

      var notice = document.getElementById('wallet-desktop-only-notice');
      if (notice) {
        var slot = notice.querySelector('[data-desktop-only-message]');
        if (slot && typeof wp.desktopOnlyMessage === 'function') {
          slot.textContent = wp.desktopOnlyMessage();
        }
        notice.hidden = false;
      }

      // DISABLED, NOT HIDDEN. The operator still needs to see that they
      // reached the right page — hiding the controls turns "wrong device"
      // into "wrong page", and they go looking for the one that works.
      //
      // `disabled` ALONE, no classes. studio-engine's button primitive already
      // ships `.btn:disabled { cursor: not-allowed; opacity: .5 }`, so the
      // opacity-50 / cursor-not-allowed this first added were duplicating a
      // rule that was already firing — verified in the browser, not assumed:
      // a bare disabled .btn computes opacity 0.5 on its own. Adding classes
      // also leaves them behind if anything ever re-enables the button.
      var buttons = document.querySelectorAll('[data-desktop-only-action]');
      for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i++) buttons[i].disabled = true;
    }

    // The buttons are rendered BELOW this partial, so the query above finds
    // nothing at parse time. Wait for the parsed document; run immediately
    // when a Turbo navigation drops us in after that point.
    if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
      document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', paintDesktopOnlyNotice);
    } else {
      paintDesktopOnlyNotice();
    }
  })();
</script>
